{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "Only 1 result/cycle families can feed a 32-request burst inside 0.5 us; pipelined_m additionally cuts the number of register stages (ceil(N/m)+2) so it is the most likely area winner. The m sweep exposes the area-vs-Fmax trade-off: large m saves FFs but lengthens the combinational chain and the latency, which the batch bound punishes."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "why": "The full-register pipeline is the safest way to satisfy the burst deadline (latency N+2, one result/cycle, shortest combinational path so highest Fmax). It anchors the fast/high-margin end of the front and gives the accuracy/area boundary a reference point against pipelined_m."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 13, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 6, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.15,
      "why": "A shared datapath would be far cheaper in area than any registered pipeline, so it is worth a cheap probe: with k=8 and N~13 it needs 32*(ceil(N/k)+3) ~ 160 cycles in 0.5 us, i.e. ~320 MHz, which is probably out of reach, but if it passes it dominates on area. Small share because the cycle count makes it a long shot."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The binding constraints are the burst deadline (32 results within 0.5 us of a 1 MHz tick) and max_abs_err <= 2^-12. Iterative is excluded analytically: 32*(N+3) cycles with N>=13 needs >1 GHz, and unrolled_k is only marginally better, so the front must come from the 1-result/cycle families. Accuracy needs roughly W>=14 (output LSB 2^-(W-2)) and N>=13 (residual angle ~2^-N), so I centre the boxes on W 13..18, N 12..18 with small guard ranges, and let the exact model find the true accuracy boundary. Area is the objective, so pipelined_m (fewer register stages) gets the largest share, with pipelined as the high-Fmax anchor and a small unrolled_k probe in case a shared datapath can still make the deadline. Later rounds should widen W/N upward for accuracy_bits > 12 (hypervolume) and map the full m/k ranges of whichever families survive."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "The feasible front is entirely one family (pipelined, 12 designs) and covers only a small part of both objective ranges: area_um2 41562..56736 against the HV reference 100000 (so the high-area/high-accuracy end is empty), and accuracy_bits 12.8..14.8 while the registry allows data_width up to 28 / n_iter up to 30 (far higher accuracy). Because the HV reference point is (area=100000, accuracy=12), the hypervolume is dominated by high-accuracy points — e.g. a feasible point near (70000, 20) would add ~2.4e5 of box area versus the current total 1.497e5 — so extending the front upward in accuracy at moderate area is the single biggest HV lever, and the current front does not reach it. The low-area end is also not proven: data_width 16 was feasible for pipelined yet no data_width-16 design is on the front, and pipelined_m (fewer registers, same 1 result/cycle) had 0 feasible in 75 evals but was never mapped in the region that works for pipelined. unrolled_k (max 4.78 MSPS) and iterative (1 result per N+3 cycles) are structurally unable to reach the 32 MSPS throughput constraint, so they are not worth budget. Therefore let code spend this round running NSGA-II over the full registry ranges of the families on the front (pipelined, plus pipelined_m as a near-front family that could lower area), seeded with the current front, to fill in the missing high-accuracy and low-area ends before the final refinement round."
  ,"next_plan": null
}
